WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.420
Legacy C APIs such as rand are familiar, but they are not appropriate for security-sensitive randomness.

2
00:00:06.520 --> 00:00:14.120
They were not designed to protect tokens, keys, nonces, session identifiers, or other values where prediction has security consequences.

3
00:00:14.400 --> 00:00:17.520
The behavior of rand can vary by implementation.

4
00:00:17.620 --> 00:00:25.300
Range, quality, period, state size, and seeding behavior are not properties you should depend on for sensitive decisions.

5
00:00:25.400 --> 00:00:29.560
Code that seems acceptable on one platform may have different behavior elsewhere.

6
00:00:29.840 --> 00:00:34.320
Small or predictable seeds produce repeatable sequences.

7
00:00:34.420 --> 00:00:41.140
Seeding a deterministic generator with a low-entropy value can make output repeat across runs or environments.

8
00:00:41.240 --> 00:00:45.320
That may be acceptable when repeatability is intentional for tests.

9
00:00:45.420 --> 00:00:49.820
It is not acceptable for secrets or access-control values.

10
00:00:50.100 --> 00:00:56.740
Simple modulo logic can also introduce range bias when the source range is not evenly divisible by the target range.

11
00:00:56.840 --> 00:01:00.360
This is a separate issue from the quality of the generator itself.

12
00:01:00.460 --> 00:01:04.220
Even a good source can be mapped poorly into a bounded range.

13
00:01:04.500 --> 00:01:11.620
For limited non-security uses, legacy APIs may be acceptable when the quality requirements are modest and documented.

14
00:01:11.720 --> 00:01:18.440
The important review rule is to flag any security-sensitive use of legacy or weak randomness APIs.

15
00:01:18.540 --> 00:01:27.180
If the value protects access, confidentiality, integrity, or protocol safety, use a trusted platform or approved library source instead.
